<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Equatorial Guinea</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Equatorial Guinea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Equatorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> is a country on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> and the only Spanish-speaking country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It consists of a mainland region, Río Muni, and islands including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> and Annobón. Pygmies are the first confirmed inhabitants of the area, followed by a migration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> groups in antiquity. The Portuguese explorer Fernando Pó explored it in 1472, and by the 1778 Treaty of El Pardo Portugal ceded territories in the Bight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It was declared Spanish Guinea during the Scramble for Africa and gained independence in 1968 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of Francisco Macías Nguema, who was overthrown in 1979 by his nephew Teodoro Obiang Nguema Mbasogo.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The country has a mainland part, Río Muni, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> to the east and south, and several islands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> lies about 40 kilometres off the coast of Cameroon, Annobón about 350 kilometres west-south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-lopez">Cape Lopez</a> in Gabon, and Corisco and the two Elobey islands in Corisco Bay. Despite the name, no part of the territory lies on the Equator: it is in the northern hemisphere except for Annobón, which is about 155 kilometres south of it. The climate is tropical, with wet and dry seasons that alternate between Río Muni and Bioko.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> migrants settled the mainland by about 500 BCE at the latest, the earliest settlements on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> date from about 530 CE, and the people of Annobón, who came originally from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, were brought by the Portuguese by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a>. Portugal colonised Bioko and Annobón in 1474, but an attempt in 1507 to found a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> plantation on Bioko failed because of Bubi hostility and fever.</p><p>Brigadier Felipe José, Count of Arjelejos, took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 21 October 1778. He died of a tropical disease on the island, and his crew, having lost more than 80 per cent of their men to sickness, mutinied and landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a>, where Portuguese authorities imprisoned them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> returned the island to Spain in 1844. Spain invested little because of epidemics, and a yellow fever outbreak in 1862 killed many white settlers, though private planters continued to found plantations. Spain left much of its mainland claim unoccupied, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> expanded at its expense. The territory became a province in 1959, and from 1960 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> attempted a partial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>.</p><p>Independence came on 12 October 1968, and Francisco Macías Nguema won the country's only free and fair election to date, in a second round. In 1970 he replaced Spanish with Fang as the official language; that was reversed in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital since January 2026 is Ciudad de la Paz, and Bata is the largest city. Spanish has been an official language since 1844, French was added in 1998 and Portuguese in 2010. The population was 1,853,559 in 2025, most of them Fang, with the Bubi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bioko">Bioko</a> the second largest group. About 93 per cent are Christian, mainly Roman Catholic. The currency is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cfa-franc">CFA franc</a>, adopted on 1 January 1985, and oil, found in the mid-1990s, became the basis of the economy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Equatorial Guinea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Africa it gave up its protectorate in Morocco in 1956 and granted <b>Equatorial Guinea</b> independence in 1968.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Exiles in <b>Equatorial Guinea</b> founded the Marxist Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe (MLSTP) in 1972, intending to create an independent nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-sao-tome-and-principe">Portuguese São Tomé and Príncipe</a></li></ul></section>
